#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41890742-709a-5aca-a2cf-296dc6d9ea7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308872} {:answer/id #uuid "6e49382a-59f6-577b-891b-a93cc6ca46ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308873} {:answer/id #uuid "d9804076-604a-550c-9aaf-e6f1d3abf5b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308874} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9f77ac-125a-552b-a163-88574b93b04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308875} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef30669-6734-5a93-a3fe-130ce4db12a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd9f77ac-125a-552b-a163-88574b93b04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308875} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e07f48-c283-5f23-84c8-e79f428261e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8723dbf-c27e-503d-97d5-3da3c89aad5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140265" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-1)y(-1)+x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=1+{t}^{2}$ and $q(t)=-5{t}^{2}+2t+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2)(k-7)+(1)(k)+(2)(k-3) \\\\  & =2k-14+k+2k-6 \\\\  & =5k-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5k-20 & =0 \\\\ 5k & =20 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ace65c7c-06a3-5407-884d-62a8b0a5f048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132597} {:answer/id #uuid "f796c9b0-cb81-5c2c-8ec7-5cd03b4718ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132598} {:answer/id #uuid "04a9082e-aae4-5d49-93bf-cc0898d8c625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132599} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2a6f32-07cd-582b-a808-f4f58cbc3f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132600} {:answer/id #uuid "1581f37c-3373-53bc-8d8b-c02e55353f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f796c9b0-cb81-5c2c-8ec7-5cd03b4718ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132598} :answer-field/id #uuid "7543960f-0192-5b05-a83f-b03d88b6817b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65ce77e2-71c5-5054-a9e0-2b1a859bf906" :question/math-academy-id "q-43981" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi +\\pi )+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ee6f458-059f-5089-8b01-9a1e6f548462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308942} {:answer/id #uuid "5a11b1ae-fada-5005-bd66-ff5c77d9a95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308943} {:answer/id #uuid "6b30ded1-ec87-5f36-b1fa-c3e4adb378ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308944} {:answer/id #uuid "029a292e-c0c8-5769-a8df-b8750573e36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308945} {:answer/id #uuid "69b702c4-fc17-56da-87b5-95928e09085c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "029a292e-c0c8-5769-a8df-b8750573e36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308945} :answer-field/id #uuid "d741525d-6f40-5084-86dd-75dd3f0c4421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb77c4e3-9a2f-52aa-a423-e70fc36097a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97361" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-2)y(-2)+x(0)y(0)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=1-2t$ and $q(t)=2-k{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-2)q(-2)+p(0)q(0)+p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(1+4)(2-4k)+(1)(2)+(1-4)(2-4k) \\\\  & =(10-20k)+2+(-6+12k) \\\\  & =6-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8729dedd-99f4-5bd4-b822-9e3fc8513768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365406} {:answer/id #uuid "b3843d0b-1803-56e8-b32f-af509f5da38c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365407} {:answer/id #uuid "56b94ba8-213a-5261-b334-5d564abf8093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365408} {:answer/id #uuid "a57ad915-f413-5d09-976d-1c9c48ce9bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365409} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ec3745-68e9-534c-8ee2-76a55a591e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56b94ba8-213a-5261-b334-5d564abf8093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365408} :answer-field/id #uuid "9996d238-92a3-5041-9c02-4500350bce67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f480f100-1995-590a-9540-47ba0d705389" :question/math-academy-id "q-97442" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)={t}^{2}$ and $y(t)=k+3t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}(k+3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{4}}{4}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{3}{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{3} & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afab6836-6d21-527f-b92e-e76b0474653c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365413} {:answer/id #uuid "14319543-aa0d-53f3-8b4c-be797169d621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365414} {:answer/id #uuid "39c1c305-2abb-5450-af8a-fa99c5e1a01a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365415} {:answer/id #uuid "d7201656-7f5d-5184-8f63-62f15521b073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365416} {:answer/id #uuid "ad619b31-88cc-54d3-b4ca-bede24ba88eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afab6836-6d21-527f-b92e-e76b0474653c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365413} :answer-field/id #uuid "22410652-8ca7-54b5-bf18-bca90f903a5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a53cb6e-220d-5e52-afd0-69dc504c17a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97449" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ and $g(t)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\pi $\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}{2}|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
